# The ledger is the record; the runtime state around it is not.
.adlc/

# Built binaries, anchored so the pattern cannot swallow cmd/adlc/.
/adlc
/adlc.exe

*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

.claude/worktrees/

# The per-run brief, written to each workspace root by internal/dispatch.
# Not covered by .adlc/ above — that matches the directory, not a file whose
# name merely starts with it — so a run doing `git add -A` committed its own
# prompt onto the branch it was about to be judged on. The decision to ignore it
# was taken and recorded (docs/decisions-overnight.md, S1-022-Q1); the one line
# it needed never landed.
.adlc-prompt.md

# The product a run builds.
#
# This repository is the control plane: ADLC is the product here, and anything
# it is pointed at to prove the pipeline is scaffolding rather than source. The
# relationship inverts wherever ADLC is USED — there the product is tracked and
# ADLC itself is what gets ignored — so neither tree ever carries the other's
# history, and nobody later has to explain why a control plane contains a
# children's website.
#
# The consequence, stated rather than discovered: with this ignored,
# a builder's "git add -A" stages nothing for an item scoped here, so the merge
# queue sees an empty branch and the item does not land. That is correct for
# this repository and wrong for one where the site IS the deliverable.
site/

# The product trees a fleet builds in. Each is its own git repository — that is
# what makes the control plane invisible to the agents working there, rather
# than merely out of their declared scope.
projects/
